Title: Journal sitter state transitions through Zotero.debug and an in-memory ring
Created: 2026-09-06
Author: claude
Blocked-by: github.com/MinhHaDuong/search-works-for-zotero#365

--- log ---
2026-09-06T00:00Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context

The author wants a debug mode that journals execution flow and critical
states, specifically to diagnose hangs and crashes -- the sitter's whole
point is to supervise Zotero's native worker, so its own fragility defeats
its purpose (author, 2026-09-06).

An initial pass (drafted with a Fable 5.1 synthesis, see the "Sitter's
Ledger" review artifact) proposed a wholly new, rotating file,
`sdt-sitter-debug.jsonl`. That conflicts with two things this project has
already settled, and should not ship as designed:

1. **A ratified project decision.** DECISIONS.md, 2026-09-05 (RATIFIED,
   experimental overnight native SDT sitter): "Failures are suppressed for
   the session by source and processor identity, **without a private
   durable ledger**." A persistent debug/failure file is exactly the durable
   ledger that ruling forbids, unless the author explicitly carves out an
   exception for it.
2. **Zotero's own platform convention.** `Zotero.debug()` is the standard
   plugin logging call, viewable via Help -> Debug Output Logging -> View
   Output. Zotero's own debug output is deliberately **not** persistent --
   per Zotero's documentation it "needs to be started/cleared for
   performance and memory reasons." Building a parallel, persistent
   channel goes against the platform's own choice for this exact class of
   diagnostic data, and it also means the author's existing habit of
   opening Debug Output Logging to look at a plugin problem would not see
   the sitter's journal at all.

## Design

- **Primary channel: `Zotero.debug()`.** Emit the sitter's state-transition
  vocabulary through it -- `admit()`, `submit()`, `progress()`, `settle()`,
  `refuse()`, `worker-idle-wait`, `dialog-open/close`, `cache-load`,
  `cache-write`, `shutdown()`, and a `heartbeat` every 60s while active
  (phase, elapsed time on the active document, pending count). No titles,
  no source paths -- same privacy rule as the existing cache.
- **In-memory ring buffer, always on, never written to disk by default.**
  2,000 records. This is what makes a hang diagnosable *after* it happens
  without violating the no-durable-ledger ruling: nothing here survives a
  crash, but the last 2,000 transitions are there the moment the author
  opens the panel. Surfaced in the dialog's diagnostics disclosure (see
  0691) with a "copy" action.
- **A single pref**, e.g. `extensions.sdt-pack-sitter.debug`, gates only
  the `trace`-level detail (heartbeats, per-probe resource readings) going
  to `Zotero.debug()` -- `error` and `state` levels always go through it,
  matching how "Report Errors" behaves versus "Debug Output" in Zotero's
  own UI.
- **If the author wants a persisted file anyway** (surviving a full Zotero
  crash, which the ring buffer cannot), that is a new, explicit ruling on
  top of the 2026-09-05 no-durable-ledger decision, not something to add
  silently in this ticket. Flag it back to the author rather than deciding
  it here.
- Hang discrimination: the panel shows "last progress N min ago" read from
  the ring; it never renders a hang verdict -- ticket 0681 owns deciding
  what a *proven* worker death is, and reads this journal as its evidence
  channel.

## Acceptance

- [ ] After a synthetic hang, the ring's tail names the active document
      identity, the last progress tick, and every heartbeat since --
      verified by reading it, not by asserting it exists.
- [ ] `Zotero.debug()` calls never throw into the sitter loop.
- [ ] Disable mid-job writes `shutdown(disable)` as the last record, with
      nothing after -- evidence the callbacks were really removed.
- [ ] No new file is written to disk by this ticket. If that changes, the
      ticket log records the author's explicit go-ahead first.
